\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Clifford algebras under scalar extension}
\date{2026-10-18}
\lean{CliffordAlgebra.equivBaseChange}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative ring in which #{2} is a unit,
#{M} any #{R}-module with quadratic form #{q}, and #{A} any
commutative #{R}-algebra. Extend the symmetric half-polar form
##{b(v,w)=\frac{q(v+w)-q(v)-q(w)}2}
to #{M_A=A\otimes_R M}, and let #{q_A} be its diagonal.
Thus
##{q_A(a\otimes v)=a^2q(v),}
where #{q(v)} on the right is mapped into #{A}. There is a
canonical #{A}-algebra isomorphism
##{\begin{aligned}
\beta_A:A\otimes_R\operatorname{Cl}_R(q)
&\longrightarrow\operatorname{Cl}_A(q_A),\\
\beta_A(a\otimes\iota_q(v))
&=a\,\iota_{q_A}(1\otimes v).
\end{aligned}}
No flatness, nondegeneracy, finite generation, or injectivity of
#{R\to A} is required. This extends the displayed field case
of [scalar extension](fcap-003V) to the declared commutative
rings, using the same \vocabk{universal property}{ca-000C}.}

\proof{
\p{The #{R}-linear map #{v\mapsto\iota_{q_A}(1\otimes v)}
has square #{q(v)1}, so the universal property extends it to
an #{R}-algebra map out of #{\operatorname{Cl}_R(q)}.
Central #{A}-scalars then extend it to #{\beta_A}. The algebra
structure on the tensor product uses
##{(a\otimes c)(a'\otimes c')=aa'\otimes cc'.}
It is well-defined because the #{R}-scalars are central.}

\p{Conversely, the balanced bilinear assignment
#{(a,v)\mapsto a\otimes\iota_q(v)} gives an #{A}-linear map
##{j:M_A\longrightarrow A\otimes_R\operatorname{Cl}_R(q).}
Write #{\iota=\iota_q} and let
##{\begin{aligned}
B_q(v,w)&=q(v+w)\\
&\quad-q(v)-q(w)\\
&=2b(v,w)
\end{aligned}}
be the full polar form. The defining square relation gives
##{\iota(v)\iota(w)+\iota(w)\iota(v)=B_q(v,w)1.}
For an arbitrary finite sum #{x=\sum_i a_i\otimes v_i},
write #{w_i=\iota(v_i)}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
j(x)^2
&=\sum_i a_i^2\otimes w_i^2\\
&+\sum_{i<j}a_i a_j\otimes
 (w_iw_j+w_jw_i)\\
&=\Bigl(\sum_i a_i^2q(v_i)\\
&\quad+\sum_{i<j}a_i a_j B_q(v_i,v_j)\Bigr)1\\
&=q_A(x)1.
\end{aligned}}
Every tensor is such a finite sum; the cross terms are part of
the square condition. Thus #{j} extends to an #{A}-algebra map
in the opposite direction. Both composites fix scalars and
vector generators. In the tensor-product algebra,
#{a\otimes1} and #{1\otimes\iota(v)} generate; in the other
algebra, the vectors #{\iota_{q_A}(x)} and scalars generate.
The composites are therefore identities.}
}

\p{Mathlib's marked equivalence has the reverse direction,
##{\operatorname{Cl}_A(q_A)
\simeq A\otimes_R\operatorname{Cl}_R(q).}
Its inverse is #{\beta_A}, and
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.ofBaseChange_tmul_ι}
gives the displayed generator formula.
\leanref{QuadraticForm.baseChange} constructs #{q_A}; its
\leanref{QuadraticForm.baseChange_tmul} computes pure tensors.
These declarations have exactly the commutative-ring and
invertible-two hypotheses above. The marker concerns the
generic algebra equivalence, not the arithmetic domain
calculations or the sheaf multiplication below.}

\p{Scalar extension is compatible with a further commutative
algebra #{A\to B}. Identify
##{\begin{aligned}
B\otimes_A(A\otimes_R M)&\longrightarrow B\otimes_R M,\\
b\otimes(a\otimes v)&\longmapsto ba\otimes v.
\end{aligned}}
The inverse inserts #{1} in the middle tensor factor.
Under this identification the direct and successive Clifford
maps both send #{b\otimes(a\otimes\iota_q(v))} to
#{ba\,\iota_{q_B}(1\otimes v)}. These elements and scalars
generate, proving equality of the algebra maps. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticForm.baseChangeBaseChange}
uses the inverse identification, from direct to successive
extension. With compatible #{R}- and #{A}-algebra structures
on #{B}, its
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.ofBaseChangeAux_baseChange}
states the same compatibility in that direction.}

\p{For an #{R}-linear form-preserving map #{P:M\to M}, put
#{P_A(a\otimes v)=a\otimes Pv}. The two orders of applying
#{\beta_A} and the induced [Clifford map](fdip-000Q) both
take #{a\otimes\iota_q(v)} to
#{a\,\iota_{q_A}(1\otimes Pv)}. Generation again proves
equivariance. In particular the [arithmetic half-turn](fdip-000U)
and its #{C_2} action commute with every allowed scalar extension.}

\p{Apply this to #{C=\operatorname{Cl}_{R_q}(q)} on the
[arithmetic base](fdip-000U) #{X_q}. Write
#{\mathcal O_{X_q}} for its structure sheaf: regular functions
on #{D_{X_q}(g)} form #{(R_q)_g}. The
\newvocab{associated module sheaf} #{\widetilde C} has
##{\Gamma(D_{X_q}(g),\widetilde C)
=C_g\simeq(R_q)_g\otimes_{R_q}C.}
Its restriction maps are the localization maps; compatible
local sections glue uniquely. This construction and the
standard-open formula are
\citet{Section 26.5 and Lemma 26.5.4, tag 01HR}{stacks2017stacks}.
Mathlib's \leanref{AlgebraicGeometry.tilde}, with its
standard-open localized-module instance, supplies the underlying
module sheaf. It does not by itself give the algebra structure.}

\p{Multiplication and unit on a standard open are
##{\frac{c}{g^m}\frac{d}{g^n}
=\frac{cd}{g^{m+n}},\qquad1=\frac11.}
The denominators are central, so these operations are
well-defined even though #{C} need not be commutative.
Restriction maps preserve them. On an arbitrary open, cover
by standard opens and multiply the restrictions of two sections.
The products agree on overlaps, so the module-sheaf axiom
glues them uniquely; the same argument glues the unit.
Associativity, additivity, the unit law and central
#{\mathcal O_{X_q}} action hold locally and hence globally.
Thus #{\widetilde C} is a sheaf of unital associative
#{\mathcal O_{X_q}}-algebras. No spectrum of the
noncommutative algebra #{C} is taken.}

\p{The isomorphisms #{\beta_{(R_q)_g}} identify these
standard-open sections with
##{\operatorname{Cl}_{(R_q)_g}(q_g).}
Scalar-tower compatibility
makes the identifications commute with restrictions. This
constructs the Clifford algebra sheaf of
#{\mathcal O_{X_q}^4} with matrix #{Q}. The half-turn acts
on every localization by the same generator formula and
commutes with restrictions, giving a #{C_2} action on the sheaf.
Restricting to #{X_{\mathrm{elim}}} or #{X_{\mathrm{obs}}}
uses exactly these base-change maps.}

\p{At the real specialization #{t=1}, put
#{q_1(x)=x^TQ(1)x} and let
#{c_i=\iota_{q_1}(\delta_i)} with the coordinate notation of
the [configuration Clifford algebra](fdip-000P). Then
##{c_i^2=3\,1,\qquad
c_ic_j+c_jc_i=2Q(1)_{ij}\,1.}
The map #{\beta_{\mathbb R}} identifies these relations with
#{\mathbb R\otimes_{R_q}C}; direct evaluation or evaluation
through either localization gives the same algebra and action.
This evaluation is not injective: it kills the nonzero element
#{t-1} of #{R_q}. The isomorphism requires no injectivity.
The [effective rank-two form](fdip-000W) has its own Clifford
algebra on #{X_{\mathrm{elim}}}; it is separate from this
rank-four construction.}
